/// EXPECTED TO WORK — currently REJECTED. (iterators stress test, 2026-07-15)
///
/// THE root blocker to a generic (any-element-type) iterator. A recursive-generic PRODUCER — a function
/// that BUILDS a generic recursive type from a generic input, recursing — fails to tie its result's
/// element type to its argument's element type. `from-list : List(a) -> Iter(a)` is inferred as
/// `_ -> Iter(_)` with TWO INDEPENDENT free vars (verified with `cdz type from-list`), instead of the
/// connected `∀a. List(a) -> Iter(a)`. The element `h` (of type `a`, destructured from `xs : List(a)`)
/// is bound into `Iter.Cons((h, from-list(t)))`, but the `Iter`'s element parameter is never unified
/// with `h`'s type across the recursion, so the result element stays a free `Var`.
///
/// SYMPTOM: a composed call `collect(from-list(xs))` (or `len(from-list(xs))`) declines with CDZ0201
/// "a generic type argument is undetermined" — BUT ONLY when the composition is instantiated at TWO OR
/// MORE distinct element types in the same program. A single instantiation compiles AND RUNS.
///
/// CHECK vs COMPILE SPLIT (diagnostically important): `cdz check` PASSES on this file — the type
/// checker accepts it. The decline surfaces at LOWERING (`cdz test` / `cdz compile`), in
/// `type_specialize` (lower.rs ~8478), when the monomorphizer tries to specialize the second
/// instantiation. So this is a LOWERING/monomorphization gap, not an inference-check rejection — the
/// two stages disagree.
///
/// MINIMAL TRIGGER (bisected precisely):
///   collect(from-list([1,2,3]))   alone            -> COMPILES + RUNS (result [1,2,3])
///   collect(from-list(["a","b"])) alone            -> COMPILES + RUNS (result ["a","b"])
///   BOTH in one program (Int64 AND String)          -> DECLINES at BOTH call sites
/// So a generic iterator works for ANY ONE element type per program; it is the SECOND concurrent
/// instantiation of the composed recursive-generic call that the monomorphizer cannot resolve.
///
/// DISCRIMINATORS (each WORKS — isolating the trigger):
///   - Single-instantiation composition compiles and runs at Int64 AND (separately) at String.
///   - A recursive-generic CONSUMER returning a CONCRETE type (`len : Lst(a) -> Int64`) composed with a
///     recursive-generic producer works at TWO instantiations — it is the producer's disconnected result
///     element that bites, surfaced when the consumer must be specialized per element type.
///   - The producer's result element IS tied when the producer is NON-recursive
///     (`single(x) = Iter.Cons((x, Iter.Nil(unit)))` is `a -> Iter(a)`).
///   - The MONOMORPHIC recursive producer works at any number of uses.
///   - BROADER THAN USER SUMS: the built-in `List` has it too — `dup-all(copy(xs))` (two recursive-
///     generic List functions composed) declines identically at two instantiations. So this is a general
///     recursive-generic MONOMORPHIZATION gap for a COMPOSED call specialized at ≥2 element types, not
///     something specific to user-defined `Iter`. (A single-instantiation composed call over either a
///     user sum or a built-in List compiles and runs.)
///   - An explicit `(t: Type)` parameter does NOT help — the decline is in the recursive-generic
///     specialization path, not the annotation surface.
///
/// ROOT (inference, pinned to the exact mechanism): `compute_def_scheme` (infer.rs ~2959) computes a
/// def's PARAM types and its RESULT type by two INDEPENDENT `type_of` calls with NO shared substitution
/// between them. For `from-list`, `solve_recursive_params` (~1874) assigns the generic param `xs` a
/// FRESH canonical var (`Ty::Var(fresh.var())`, the "generic position" branch ~1877) stored in
/// `db.param_types`; then `type_of(body)` produces the result `Iter <different-fresh-var>` — the two
/// vars are never unified, because nothing types the body's return construction `Iter.Cons(h, …)`
/// against the param's var. `collect_free_vars` then finds BOTH and quantifies them separately →
/// `from-list : ∀a b. List(a) -> Iter(b)` (verified: `cdz type from-list` → `(-> _ (Iter _))`). With ONE
/// instantiation the outer call's context pins the loose result var; with TWO, `type_specialize`
/// (lower.rs ~8478) has no single value to bind it to and declines at each site.
///
/// TRIED + REVERTED (2026-07-15): mirroring `apply_scheme_to_args`' "seed the instantiation counter above
/// the arg vars + DON'T freshen" trick inside `apply_type`'s scheme-application loop. It REGRESSED a
/// nested-Result-pattern gate case (the freshening there is load-bearing for the `(Some (None))`
/// occurs-check) and did NOT fix the scheme — because the disconnect is in `compute_def_scheme`/
/// `solve_recursive_params` (the scheme is built there, independent of `apply_type`), NOT in application.
///
/// DESIRED FIX LOCUS: `compute_def_scheme`/`solve_recursive_params` must type the body's RETURN
/// construction under the SAME env+subst that holds the param vars, so the result's element var IS the
/// param's — a real fixpoint/shared-subst extension to the scheme solver (the corpus never exercises a
/// recursive-generic PRODUCER, so this path is genuinely untested, not a regression). Then
/// `from-list : ∀a. List(a) -> Iter(a)` and `collect(from-list(xs))` type-checks for ANY element type.
///
/// WORKAROUND `src/iter.cdz` ships: a MONOMORPHIC `Iter` over `Int64` (the element type is fixed in the
/// type declaration, so the producer's result element is concrete and nothing floats). A generic
/// iterator is gated on this fix.

type Iter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, Iter(a))

/// A recursive-generic PRODUCER — should be `∀a. List(a) -> Iter(a)`, inferred `(-> _ (Iter _))` with
/// the result element DISCONNECTED from the argument element.
def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => Iter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => Iter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

/// A recursive-generic CONSUMER returning a generic result.
def collect(it) = match it with
  | Iter.Nil(_) => []
  | Iter.Cons(h, rest) => List.concat([h], collect(rest))

def int-list-eq(a, b) = match a with
  | [] => (match b with | [] => true | _ => false)
  | [x, .. xs] => (match b with | [] => false | [y, .. ys] => if x == y then int-list-eq(xs, ys) else false)

def str-list-eq(a, b) = match a with
  | [] => (match b with | [] => true | _ => false)
  | [x, .. xs] => (match b with | [] => false | [y, .. ys] => if x == y then str-list-eq(xs, ys) else false)

/// EACH of these compiles + RUNS on its own; together (two instantiations of the composed
/// recursive-generic `collect(from-list(…))`) BOTH call sites decline.
@test
def ints() =
  if int-list-eq(collect(from-list([1, 2, 3])), [1, 2, 3]) then unit else trap("ints")

@test
def strings() =
  if str-list-eq(collect(from-list(["a", "bb", "ccc"])), ["a", "bb", "ccc"]) then unit else trap("strings")

export { Iter.* }

/// TRIAGE 2026-07-16 (corpus-bugfix): deep LOWERING/monomorphization gap (type_specialize, 2-instantiation tie). Owned by v-inference — matches the deep-deferred re-entrancy queued item. Parked; not a quick fix. Re-surface to v-inference when it picks up the self-nested-producer scheme-solve work.
